            <div xml:id="d336">

                <pb facs="0538" n="394" xml:id="pg394"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/601: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Neville</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-05T17:00:00-05:00">December 5, 1929—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>124. On December 2, in conversation, I asked the Japanese Ambassador to make
                    clear to Baron Shidehara that our recent action in regard to Russia and China
                    was not taken from any desire to intrude into Manchurian affairs but to save the
                    Kellogg-Briand Pact from losing its strength and force. He said he understood
                    perfectly and would make this clear to his Government. The foregoing merely for
                    your information.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
